The keystones are the twenty cut gems set out by the host of the [[Phenomena/Pilgrimage|pilgrimage]] at [[Geography/Calaria/Ragged Roost|Ragged Roost]] as the price of a place aboard his strider. He showed one to the guests from the scaffolding: a faceted cerulean stone floating in an embossed golden box. Nineteen more, he said, had been placed in or around [[Geography/Calaria/Hallia/index|Hallia]]. A keystone buys passage for its bearer and one vehicle with whatever it will carry, and two keystones buy two vehicles. The only rule is that the bearer hold a keystone in his hands when the strider leaves, six months after the night of the announcement; the host said he cared nothing for how it was come by or who found it.

Guests could learn more by trading at the party or by taking on the games along the street. The Party won three questions from a harlequin by surviving the trial by combat in the train yard, and spent them on the keystone nearest the [[Sin and Tonic]]. It is number 14, held by a [[Phenomena/Hearts|Heart]] calling itself Rapid Connection, and the person most likely to lead them to that Heart is [[People/Hogan Voss Darkmoor|Archmechanic Hogan Voss Darkmoor]] of the Darkmoors. No one in the Party had heard of either.

The Party learned more at the games along the street. From the association game on the fairy-ring path they learned that the holder of keystone 14 is a collector of fine art, and the fortune teller gave the kind: paintings, oil or acrylic on canvas. From the dice table they learned that the holder of keystone 15 would trade the keystone for a relic of the [[Org/Iridescent Church/index|Iridescent Church]], a reliquary or a holy book, and from the prism game that Second-Among-Equals [[People/Thistle Monroe|Thistle Monroe]] is the one most likely to knowingly lead to it.

The [[Org/Iconoclasm/People/Anouk Marchal|Marchal]] twins spent two questions of their own on the same keystone and learned that four parties hunt it in all, counting the twins and the Party; the other two are [[People/Sbrtlby Jotes|Sbrtlby Jotes]] and his hired henchmen, and [[Org/Concordia/People/Crawford Reeves|Crawford Reeves]] and [[Org/Concordia/People/Louis Maller|Louis Maller]] of [[Org/Concordia|Concordia]]. The twins mean to kill whoever takes it first.

On 24 February the Party killed [[People/Sbrtlby Jotes|Sbrtlby Jotes]] and his henchmen at his drug works at 414 Anchor Street in [[Geography/Calaria/Hallia/Deosil|Deosil]], leaving [[Org/Concordia|Concordia]] the only rival still hunting keystone 14.

On their next visit the Party took keystone 14 from [[Org/Prime Meridian/Rapid Connection|Rapid Connection]], trading the [[People/Kellum Quinn|Kellum Quinn]] *Second Meaning* for it and for the waiver of all Prime Meridian fees. They hold it now. With [[People/Sbrtlby Jotes|Sbrtlby Jotes]] dead and the two [[Org/Concordia/People/Crawford Reeves|Concordia]] [[Org/Concordia/People/Louis Maller|agents]] killed the same day, no rival hunter was left to stand between them and the stone.

The Party now keeps keystone 14 in a lead-lined safe set in cement under the floor of the room the [[Org/Iconoclasm/People/Anouk Marchal|Marchal]] twins share at the [[Sin and Tonic]]. Lead or running water will block a simple locating spell, but neither stops a caster who names the stone itself.
